 5. 🛢️ Trump&#x27;s refinery-for-refinery deal 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Ukraine's President Volodymyr Zelensky meets with President Trump on the sidelines of UNGA in Manhattan in September. Photo: Brendan Smialowski/AFP via Getty Images President Trump's announcement yesterday about an "energy ceasefire" between Russia and Ukraine referred to a potential deal that includes each side stopping attacks on oil refineries — and he expects Volodymyr Zelensky to abide by it, a White House official told Axios' Barak Ravid. 

 The official said if attacks on oil refineries cease, a deal to stop hitting power plants and energy grids could come next. Why it matters: Trump appears to be single-handedly trying to will a partial ceasefire into existence. But for now, Russia hasn't accepted any kind of ceasefire — and Ukraine wants one that forbids attacks on power plants.

 👀 Reality check: The "refinery for refinery" proposal, as the official dubbed it, is lopsided.

 A Ukrainian official told Axios that the country has a single oil refinery, which the Russians have attacked multiple times and isn't fully functional. Russia, by contrast, has a vast network of refineries. Six senators, including four Republicans , have called on Trump to "immediately reverse course" on the plan. Read on . 

 
 
